/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  The benzene is 23 mol% of the feed and the water 68 %: far past any
  solubility, so the two liquids are not a marginal effect to be teased out
  of a stability test -- they are the obvious physical state of this mixture,
  which is what a first case demonstrating them should be.

  Ethanol at 9 mol% partitions between them.  It is also the co-solvent that
  would CLOSE the miscibility gap above ~40 mol%; at this loading two liquids
  still exist, and the declared model does not know why.  That limit is the
  approximation named in controlDict, made visible by a component whose
  amount matters.

  Acetic acid is a trace: enough to speciate and set a pH, not enough to
  matter to the split.  It is here so the case is REACTIVE -- without it the
  aqueous phase would have no chemistry and this would be an ordinary
  three-phase molecular flash, which the engine could already do.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
componentMolarFlows
{
    water          60.0  kmol/h;
    benzene        20.0  kmol/h;
    ethanol         8.0  kmol/h;
    aceticAcid      0.1  kmol/h;
}
T               313.15 K;
P               35464 Pa;
